# TypeScript Standards

## Strict Mode
- `strict: true` is enabled in tsconfig — never use `any` type, use `unknown` and narrow instead
- Always provide explicit return types for exported functions
- Use `satisfies` operator to validate object shapes without widening types

## Type Patterns
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes that may be extended, `type` for unions, intersections, and aliases
- Use discriminated unions for state variants (e.g., loading/success/error)
- Avoid `enum` — use `const` objects with `as const` or union literal types instead
- Re-export all shared types from `types/index.ts`

## Nullability
- Prefer explicit `| undefined` over optional properties when the absence is semantically meaningful
- Use nullish coalescing (`??`) over logical OR (`||`) for default values
- Never use non-null assertion (`!`) — narrow with type guards instead
